hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the first  sets of issues that Cicero considers in  his fragmentary work on fate has to do  with a type of determinism that he here  associates with nature's the natures of  places and the natures that people have  that we often call using an  old-fashioned word temperament and the  idea here is that growing up or being  born or living in a certain place would  have effects upon a person that would  would in some way determine their  actions would contribute to their  destiny or their fate and that's the  location that's where they live then we  can also talk about their their body  itself certain bodies would have  tendencies towards certain kinds of  behaviors and many people even in our  own time do in fact think of matters in  this way that if you're born in this  place or if you're born with this  certain set of temperaments or we might  even talk about brain states that you  inevitably are going to do or not do  certain things you'll make certain  choices and all of that will be  determined or faded something that you  cannot do otherwise there's a sort of  necessity that comes along with them now  what is the the basic argument here  Cicero says well we do in fact observe  differences in the nature's of  localities of loci in in Latin right and  what he means by there if you look at  the examples that he's bringing up  there's Athens as a city and then  there's Thebes another city and region  to the north of Athens and they each  have their own characteristics so here  in America  we might talk about the difference  between growing up in the city or in the  countryside or in the Midwest versus  growing up in the muggy south you know  we could talk about humidity and heat  and all these sorts of influences and  what they do to people and you know if  you think about for example seasonal  affective disorder right an issue for  those of us who live in places that used  to have quite harsh winters like  Wisconsin or if you think about the  people who live in places where the Sun  doesn't shine very often who you know  have similar issues like those living in  Seattle maybe there's there's actually  something to this many people would say  so he says we see the wide difference  between natural characters of different  localities that not to arrive right  he says some areas are healthy others  unhealthy and this might be because of a  whole different set of circumstances  some of which could have to do with  weather  some of which might have to do with  parasites some of which might have to do  with all sorts of other factors as well  and once we introduce human beings into  the equation and pollution we have all  sorts of other things you could think it  also about the instances of lead  poisoning in some of our large American  cities in poor neighborhoods where  piping is still sometimes lead and lead  paint has played a role in damaging  people's brains  he doesn't have those as examples he  talks about some people being phlegmatic  and overcharged with moisture others  being parched and dried up  he says Athens has a rarefied climate  which is thought to cause a sharpness of  wit above the average in the population  so he's giving a what we call a  geographical deterministic argument for  Athenian culture whereas at Thebes the  climate is dense and so the Thebans are  stout and sturdy now there is some  geographical determinism and there is  some effect on some things again you  know think about the example of lead  which the ancients unfortunately didn't  know about and poison themselves with by  using think about the influence of lead  or other metals upon brain development  maybe there is something to this but as  Cicero points out that is at a sort of  large big-picture very high view level  of determinism it doesn't actually  determine our individual actions so as  he's going to point out the rarified air  of Athens will not enable a student to  choose between the lectures of Zeno  arcus allows and Theophrastus Zeno the  stoic arcus allows the academic skeptic  and Theophrastus the friend and follower  of Aristotle who led his school the  dense air of Thebes will not make a man  try to win a race at Nemea rather than  at Corinth when we get to the level of  individual choices or even very  important choices deciding where you're  gonna go and compete or deciding which  school you're gonna study is a choice  that determines other choices the  climate doesn't actually Fate you to do  things that way you might say well the  locality could what if the Stoics are  the only people who are available in the  library that's a function of the library  that's not so much a function of the  place and web  and climate or anything like that says  carry the distinction further can the  nature of the locality cause us to take  a walk in Pompey's porch rather than in  the campus so this is quite important  Cicero will use this example of walking  or sitting or standing for what he's  going to call voluntary motions and the  geographical or climate or whatever it's  going to be determinism if there is any  such thing doesn't actually extend down  to the level of these individual  particular actions and choices that we  make so we can say that there could be  some propensity or some conditioning  because of the locality but it's not  actually going to be determining us in  any full sense he also talks about the  condition of the heavenly bodies and he  says that those can also influence  people as well  he says the the condition of the  heavenly bodies is not going to  influence everything so you know what  sign you're born under where the planets  are any of that sort of stuff it might  determine some large scale matters but  it's not actually going to decide for  you that you will do this thing at that  time  when we get to temperaments we get to  something that's less about the  environment more about who we are as  individuals and it's very clear that  different people do in fact have if not  temperaments if not something quite so  fixed they certainly have differences  from each other  dissimilarities right and he says there  are temperaments of humans that differ  he's talking about the natures the  natures of human beings differ from each  other and he says these dissimilarities  could in fact result from different  natural and antecedent causes now those  are some technical terms that were used  by metaphysics  particularly the Stoics to talk about  the things that make things happen  causes and effects we're not going to  get into what natural and antecedent  causes are here because we'll talk about  that in a separate lecture but it is  important that he uses this term several  times antecedent causes are those which  precede right they are there and then  they help to produce some sort of effect  a natural cause would be something a bit  closer more primary cause and what are  the dissimilarities that he's talking  about he gives you some examples he says  some like sweet things others slightly  bitter things some are like sancious  other prone to anger or cruel or proud  of the others shrinkin horror from vices  of that sor